00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:27.200
themselves or the Confederacy, but only for their belief in me. Do I have the right to ask that of

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:29.980
them if now their sacrifice is...

00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:33.750
No avail. It's all so simple.

00:30:34.730 --> 00:30:36.750
How easily I could be rid of this

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:39.930
and at rest. I wanted to

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:41.910
ride along the line and all

00:30:41.910 --> 00:30:45.670
will be over. It is our duty

00:30:45.670 --> 00:30:48.680
to live. What will become of the women

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:50.360
and children of the South if

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:52.540
we are not here to protect them?

00:30:57.080 --> 00:30:59.980
Those people seem to be all around

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:04.100
I can see there are fires on the distant bridge.

00:31:07.320 --> 00:31:11.480
That man is out there somewhere.

00:31:17.830 --> 00:31:19.830
May God's will be done.

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:00.180
Thank you.

00:33:09.050 --> 00:33:10.290
General, just come from the front.

00:33:10.490 --> 00:33:11.590
The message from General Gordon.

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:12.460
Proceed, General.

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:14.820
He says to tell you he has fought his corps to a frazzle.

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:16.520
His final words were,

00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:19.740
I fear I can do nothing unless I'm heavily supported by Longstreet's corps.

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:27.290
And then I fear there's nothing left for me to do but go and see General Grant.

00:33:28.590 --> 00:33:29.970
And I had rather die.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:31.200
A thousand deaths.

00:33:31.580 --> 00:33:34.380
Sir, what will they say if we surrender the army in the field?

00:33:34.780 --> 00:33:37.560
Oh, I'm sure they'll say hard things about us, Colonel.

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:41.300
They won't understand how we were overwhelmed by numbers,

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:43.280
but that's not the question, Colonel.

00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:46.400
The question is, is it right to surrender the army?

00:33:46.880 --> 00:33:49.910
If it is right, then I will take all responsibility.

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:28.280
5.

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:42.440
Lieutenant General, this is S. Grant, commanding the United States.

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:10.200
Better read it out loud, Rollins.

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:14.360
Lieutenant General U.S. Grant, commanding U.S. Armies.

00:35:15.210 --> 00:35:17.670
General, I ask a suspension of hostilities

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:21.700
pending the adjustment of the terms of surrender of this army

00:35:21.700 --> 00:35:25.400
in the interview requested in my former communication today.

00:35:26.030 --> 00:35:28.750
Very respectfully, your obedient servant R.E. Lee, General.

00:35:30.960 --> 00:35:32.060
How will that do, Wallace?

00:35:33.700 --> 00:35:35.940
I think that will do.

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:38.630
Better take down a reply.

00:35:44.210 --> 00:35:44.510
Yes, sir.

00:35:47.160 --> 00:35:51.830
General Robert E. Lee, commanding Confederate States Army.

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:57.650
Your note to this day is, but of this moment, received.

00:36:00.440 --> 00:36:02.300
It's 11.50 a.m. Put that towel off.

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:09.170
In consequence of having passed from the Richmond and Lynchburg Road

00:36:09.170 --> 00:36:10.730
to the Farmville and Lynchburg Road,

00:36:11.830 --> 00:36:17.580
I am at this riding presently about four miles west of Walker's Church.

00:36:17.950 --> 00:36:20.350
We'll push forward to the front for the purpose of meeting you.

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:25.290
No dissent on this road as to where you wish the interview to take place.

00:36:26.250 --> 00:36:28.320
We'll meet me. Respectfully yours.

00:36:30.120 --> 00:36:34.100
Remediant servant, Mrs. S. Grant, Lieutenant General.

00:36:44.930 --> 00:36:46.440
Remember that headache, Wallen?

00:36:46.980 --> 00:36:47.360
Yes, sir.

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:49.860
But it just disappeared.

00:38:07.290 --> 00:38:08.960
Hi Sheridan, how are you?

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:09.840
First rate, sir.

00:38:10.950 --> 00:38:11.590
I'll be over there.

00:38:12.660 --> 00:38:14.080
His army is down in that valley.

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:16.080
I'm in that house, waiting to surrender.

00:38:16.670 --> 00:38:18.530
Well, let's go see him.

00:39:00.190 --> 00:39:00.290
Yeah.

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:30.780
Thank you.

00:40:15.960 --> 00:40:20.090
General. General Grant, sir. Please, sir. Sit down. Sit down.

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:35.040
I met you once before, General, when we were serving in Mexico.

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:41.180
You had come over from General Scott's headquarters to visit Garland's brigade, to which I then belonged.

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:47.650
I always remembered your appearance. I think I should have recognized you anywhere.

00:40:48.500 --> 00:40:59.960
I remember meeting you on that occasion, but all through this war I tried to recall how you looked, but I was...

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:01.940
Not able to recall a single feature.

00:41:02.750 --> 00:41:06.100
Yes, well, Mexico was a wrong time ago,

00:41:07.510 --> 00:41:10.300
and I only spoke to you for a short time.

00:41:11.150 --> 00:41:13.670
I was just a young, unbearded lieutenant then.

00:41:14.490 --> 00:41:16.730
It was just outside the city of Mexico.

00:41:16.730 --> 00:41:18.490
I was standing beside some wagons,

00:41:18.490 --> 00:41:20.650
and you rode up and we chatted for a short while.

00:41:22.050 --> 00:41:22.530
Yes.

00:41:23.810 --> 00:41:26.230
I suppose, General Grant, sir,

00:41:26.370 --> 00:41:29.870
that the object of our present meeting is fully understood.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:38.810
I ask to meet with you to ascertain upon what terms you will receive the surrender of my army.

00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:40.000
Of course, General.

00:41:41.020 --> 00:41:48.440
I assure you, General, that the terms I propose are those stated substantially in my letter of yesterday.

00:41:48.960 --> 00:41:53.040
Simply, your officers and men are to lay down their arms

00:41:53.040 --> 00:41:56.540
and not to take them up again during the continuance of the war

00:41:56.540 --> 00:41:59.180
unless duly and properly exchanged.

00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:06.210
Those are about the conditions which I expected would be proposed.

00:42:07.390 --> 00:42:11.330
May I suggest, sir, that you commit to writing those terms

00:42:11.330 --> 00:42:15.470
so that they may be formally recorded and acted upon?

00:42:16.320 --> 00:42:16.940
Certainly, sir.

00:42:30.410 --> 00:42:59.970
Brothers, armies of the United States.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:05.570
States, Appomattox Courthouse, Virginia, April 9th, 1865. General Robert E. Lee, commanding

00:43:05.570 --> 00:43:10.380
Confederate States Army. General, in accordance with the substance of my letter to you of the

00:43:10.380 --> 00:43:14.750
eighth instant, I propose to receive the surrender of the Army of Northern Virginia

00:43:14.750 --> 00:43:20.660
on the following terms, to wit, roles of all the officers and men, to be made in duplicate,

00:43:21.340 --> 00:43:26.860
one copy to be given to an officer, to be designated by me, the other to be retained by such

00:43:26.860 --> 00:43:29.260
officer or officers as you may designate.

00:43:30.280 --> 00:43:35.340
The officers to give their individual paroles not to take up arms against the government of the United States

00:43:35.340 --> 00:43:42.180
until properly exchanged, and each company or regimental commander to sign a like parole for the men of his command.

00:43:42.900 --> 00:43:46.610
The arms, artillery, and public property are to be packed and stacked,

00:43:46.880 --> 00:43:49.860
and turned over to the officers appointed by me to receive them.

00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:54.750
This will not embrace the sidearms of the officers, nor their private horses or baggage.

00:43:55.380 --> 00:43:59.970
This done, officers and men will be allowed to return to their homes, not to...

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:05.280
be disturbed by United States authority so long as they observe their paroles and the laws in

00:44:05.280 --> 00:44:10.000
force where they may reside. Very respectfully, U.S. Grant, Lieutenant General.

00:44:30.480 --> 00:44:30.700
Thank you.

00:45:22.430 --> 00:45:29.980
You did intend, General, to put in the word exchange after properly, did you?

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:34.610
Yes, sir. Yes, I did, General. Just no oversight.

00:45:37.070 --> 00:45:41.820
With your permission, sir, I will mark where the word should be inserted.

00:45:42.620 --> 00:45:43.300
Please do, General.

00:46:20.320 --> 00:46:29.970
You may not be aware, General, but in the Confederate Army, the cavalrymen and artillerymen own their own horses.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:40.320
I understand that the men will be permitted to retain them as the terms are written

00:46:40.320 --> 00:46:58.080
they will not it is clear that only officers horses and property are mentioned for general

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:11.800
I know that most of your men are small farmers and they will need their horses to put in a crop and to carry them and their families through the next winter.

00:47:13.520 --> 00:47:24.880
Though I will not rewrite the terms, I will instruct my officers to let any man of your army who claims a mule or horse take the animal home.

00:47:25.740 --> 00:47:28.180
This will have a happy effect on the men.

00:48:00.990 --> 00:48:01.610
Orderly.

00:48:01.900 --> 00:48:02.440
Yes, General.

00:49:34.960 --> 00:49:41.040
We've lived through the war. There's nobody trying to shoot us, nobody for us to shoot at.

00:49:42.290 --> 00:49:45.810
Our country's gone. Our very entity seems to be destroyed.

00:49:47.010 --> 00:49:54.200
We're no longer soldiers and have no orders to obey, nothing to do and nowhere to go.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:08.580
in various ways the agonizing emotion that shake their souls and break their hearts some cry like

00:50:08.580 --> 00:50:16.760
children others sit on the ground with faces buried in hands quietly sobbing others struck

00:50:16.760 --> 00:50:23.140
dumb and with blanched faces seem to strain their eyes to catch the form of some awful horror

00:50:23.550 --> 00:50:29.660
that suddenly looms before them not a sound of trumpet more nor roll of drum

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:34.260
not a cheer, nor a word, nor a whisper of vainglorying,

00:50:34.660 --> 00:50:37.780
nor a motion of man standing again at the order,

00:50:38.080 --> 00:50:40.270
but an awed stillness, rather.

00:50:41.800 --> 00:50:45.110
We wonder at that mysterious dispensation

00:50:45.110 --> 00:50:49.000
whereby the pathway of the kingdom of love on earth

00:50:49.280 --> 00:50:52.080
must needs be cut through by the sword,

00:50:52.200 --> 00:50:54.820
and why it must be that by such things

00:50:54.820 --> 00:50:59.540
as we had seen and done and suffered and lost and won.

00:51:00.830 --> 00:51:05.780
A step is taken in the homeward march of man.

00:53:06.270 --> 00:53:09.610
Perhaps the searching of our own morality is in order.

00:53:10.300 --> 00:53:10.900
Come, Andrew.

00:53:12.390 --> 00:53:13.810
Anyway, okay, I'm ready to roll tape.

00:53:13.850 --> 00:53:14.390
No, no, no.

00:53:14.770 --> 00:53:15.550
I'll give you all the credit.

00:53:15.790 --> 00:53:17.010
No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.

00:53:18.880 --> 00:53:19.160
Okay.

00:53:20.490 --> 00:53:20.530
Okay.

00:53:20.770 --> 00:53:24.080
Do you like the music?
